  "microcalLoadFailed": "Micronutrients could not be loaded",
  "microcalSourceEuNrv": "EU label value",
  "microcalAboutVitaminA": "A fat-soluble vitamin, counted in µg retinol equivalents: retinol from animal foods, and beta-carotene from plants at a sixth of its weight. Found in liver, eggs, butter, cheese, carrots, sweet potatoes and dark leafy greens. Beta-carotene is absorbed better with some fat in the same meal.",
  "microcalAboutVitaminD": "A fat-soluble vitamin, in µg (1 µg is 40 IU). Few foods contain it: oily fish such as salmon, sardines and mackerel, egg yolks and fortified foods. The skin also makes it in sunlight, which this log does not count.",
  "microcalAboutVitaminE": "A fat-soluble vitamin, counted in mg alpha-tocopherol. Found in vegetable oils such as sunflower and olive oil, nuts, seeds and avocado.",
  "microcalAboutVitaminK": "A fat-soluble vitamin, in µg: K1 from plants and K2 from fermented and animal foods. Found in dark leafy greens such as spinach and kale, broccoli, vegetable oils and cheese. Absorbed better with some fat in the same meal.",
  "microcalAboutVitaminC": "A water-soluble vitamin, also called ascorbic acid, in mg. Found in peppers, citrus fruit, kiwi, strawberries, broccoli and tomatoes. Heat and long storage reduce it, and boiling moves part of it into the water. Vitamin C in the same meal raises the absorption of iron from plant foods.",
  "microcalAboutThiamin": "Vitamin B1, water-soluble, in mg. Found in whole grains, pork, legumes, nuts and seeds. Part of it is lost in cooking water. EFSA sets the reference per unit of energy, so it follows your daily calorie goal.",
  "microcalAboutRiboflavin": "Vitamin B2, water-soluble, in mg. Found in milk and dairy, eggs, meat, almonds and mushrooms. Light breaks it down.",
  "microcalAboutNiacin": "Vitamin B3, water-soluble, in mg. Found in meat, poultry, fish, peanuts and whole grains. The body also makes some from tryptophan, an amino acid in protein, which food tables do not include. EFSA sets the reference per unit of energy, so it follows your daily calorie goal.",
  "microcalAboutVitaminB6": "A water-soluble vitamin, in mg. Found in poultry, fish, potatoes, chickpeas, bananas and whole grains.",
  "microcalAboutFolate": "Vitamin B9, water-soluble, counted in µg dietary folate equivalents: folic acid from supplements and fortified foods counts 1.7 times, as it is absorbed better than the folate in food. Found in leafy greens, legumes, asparagus, liver and oranges. Boiling loses a large part of it.",
  "microcalAboutVitaminB12": "A water-soluble vitamin, in µg. Found almost only in animal foods (meat, fish, eggs, milk and dairy) and in fortified foods. Only a small amount is absorbed from any single meal.",
  "microcalAboutCalcium": "A mineral, in mg. Found in milk, yogurt, cheese, sardines with their bones, almonds, tahini and some leafy greens. Oxalates in spinach and phytates in bran and legumes lower its absorption from those foods.",
  "microcalAboutPhosphorus": "A mineral, in mg. Found in most protein foods (dairy, meat, fish, eggs, legumes, nuts and whole grains) and in phosphate additives in processed foods.",
  "microcalAboutMagnesium": "A mineral, in mg. Found in whole grains, nuts, seeds, legumes, cocoa and leafy greens. Its upper level applies to supplements only.",
  "microcalAboutPotassium": "A mineral, in mg. Found in vegetables, fruit such as bananas and apricots, potatoes, legumes, milk and coffee.",
  "microcalAboutIron": "A trace element, in mg. Heme iron in meat and fish is absorbed far better than non-heme iron in legumes, grains and greens. Vitamin C in the same meal raises non-heme iron absorption; tea, coffee and calcium in the same meal lower it.",
  "microcalAboutZinc": "A trace element, in mg. Found in meat, shellfish, cheese, legumes, nuts, seeds and whole grains. Phytates in whole grains and legumes lower its absorption; the EFSA reference here assumes a moderate phytate intake.",
  "microcalAboutCopper": "A trace element, in mg. Found in liver, shellfish, nuts, seeds, cocoa, legumes and whole grains.",
  "microcalAboutManganese": "A trace element, in mg. Found in whole grains, nuts, legumes, leafy greens and tea.",
  "microcalAboutSelenium": "A trace element, in µg. Found in Brazil nuts, fish, seafood, meat, eggs and grains; the amount in plant foods depends on the soil they grew in.",
  "microcalAboutIodine": "A trace element, in µg. Found in fish, seafood, seaweed, milk, eggs and iodised salt; the amount in milk and vegetables depends on farming and soil. Iodised salt added at home is not counted.",
